{
  "decisions": [
    {
      "item_id": "youtube-wMQB33rM28I",
      "version_id": "91b5117a082251bc859d7cb81ba933c439e1b0450075c2bf98b2b8112bb7d58e",
      "decision": "accept",
      "reason": "Accept as an operational tutorial under revision 2, not evidence of measured improvement. Supplied publication metadata dates it October 11, within the inclusive September 15–October 14, 2026 UTC window. Its useful contribution is a small instruction-optimization procedure: the model remains unchanged (s00010–s00013, [51.34–69.6s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=51)), grading is separated from editable instructions (s00053–s00058, [257.9–284.7s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=257)), and final tasks are withheld from optimization (s00140–s00146, [645.86–674.34s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=645)). The proposed budget totals 48 worker trials before optimizer and setup costs; this is planning arithmetic, not observed expenditure (s00160–s00170, [737.14–789.02s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=737)). No inspectable execution logs, achieved baseline/held-out scores, ablations or independent replication accompany the supplied tutorial; hypothetical outcomes cannot establish gains (s00124–s00129 and s00147–s00157). Defer the contradictory automatic-transcript passage s00105 at 489.3–493.54s pending original-audio checking; do not attribute cherry-picking advice to the speaker. Adjacent s00103–s00104 separately support fixed repeat counts. Membership and donation appeals establish a channel monetization interest, not a demonstrated vendor sponsorship (s00000–s00003; s00194–s00195, [889.18–899.9s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wMQB33rM28I&t=889)). Useful for student evaluation design; no demonstrated new capability.",
      "piece_ids": ["02", "03"]
    },
    {
      "item_id": "youtube-R9momwXV9w4",
      "version_id": "81ddd8b2230f42d36bc234d2235a06343303c4274511e44bb5cd8b1409bafab7",
      "decision": "defer",
      "reason": "September 24 publication metadata is within September 15–October 14, 2026 UTC, but the relevant new evaluation claims lack supplied original supporting documents. The commentator reports internal research-task scores and a replacement threshold (s00075–s00082, [426.72–471.92s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=426)); no task inventory, baseline protocol, compute budget, hardware, held-out controls or reproducibility artifacts are supplied. The reported absence of sustained twofold acceleration (s00072–s00074, [408.72–426.72s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=408)) and an external evaluator's purported probability of that acceleration (s00093–s00095, [527.6–542.88s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=527)) require comparison of definitions, periods and uncertainty, not averaging or a declaration of contradiction. The 2024 commitments and July 2026 revisions discussed at s00085–s00099 are older events, not September developments. These issues could serve evidence scrutiny, but the video is secondary commentary dependent on unavailable-in-this-packet system cards and evaluations. Its creator also promotes their own benchmark (s00254, [1374.64–1379.52s](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R9momwXV9w4&t=1374)); financial ties are unspecified. Lab claims have institutional interests, and excerpts of lab researchers do not supply independent replication. Defer pending original evidence; broader speculative forecasts add little to students' inspectable experiments.",
      "piece_ids": []
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ead4cdfb34d1b428c1cd760b",
      "version_id": "2440cc1580de805fb537ea10d57c87f518177ce7fa5c4cf63e2260a65098b0dc",
      "decision": "accept",
      "reason": "Accept narrowly as a scientific-practitioner account contrasting physical and computational experiments. October 8 publication metadata falls within September 15–October 14, 2026 UTC; the recent contribution is the account's operational detail, not proof that its undated lab events happened within this window. Founders describe noisy telemetry (s00008, [169–288s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=169,288)), targeted automation with full autonomy explicitly a non-goal (s00230, [3046–3099s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=3046,3099)), and preserving scientific-process lineage (s00267, [3493–3524s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=3493,3524)). These support attributed examples and the editorial inference that students should automate measurable bottlenecks while retaining provenance. Preserve the correction: the host suggests an unambiguous observable, but the guests explicitly describe remaining phase ambiguity and replicates (s00072–s00078, [1377–1438s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=1377,1438)). No released datasets, controlled baselines, held-out results, ablations, quantified budgets or independent replication substantiate superior performance. Founders have a commercial interest, made explicit in customer deployment discussion (s00341, [4506–4613s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/219146438/4de3baad7036438bcc048cc532556c6a.mp3#t=4506,4613)). Their Generalist appearance is the same interested source, not corroboration; independent researcher assessment remains missing.",
      "piece_ids": ["02", "03"]
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ade5c65097bf35b3d1cb467e",
      "version_id": "5a82503b8b0e7e873cc96ddaa5b89b9957e647009d50cff4f549b940594dfeef",
      "decision": "accept",
      "reason": "Accept for a limited additional evidence-scrutiny role: a concrete cost claim students can learn to interrogate. September 29 publication metadata is within September 15–October 14, 2026 UTC; dates of the underlying work are unspecified. A founder describes an estimated annual analysis cost at maximum second-lab throughput of roughly $30 million and compression to below $1 million (s01100–s01116, [2613–2652.46s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/215855083/2affdef5ab1fade3557377735a882b58.mp3#t=2613,2652.46)). Treat this as a founder's account of projected economics, not audited savings or a general efficiency result: model identities, hardware, workload, accuracy parity, training expense, evaluation splits and replication are absent. The student implication is an editorial inference: compare total costs at matched quality and workload before adopting specialization. Preserve the throughput qualification: the introduction asserts thousands of experiments daily (s00023–s00024, 106.28–116.38s), while the detailed discussion frames that scale as a future objective (s00746–s00750, [1838.24–1849.86s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/215855083/2affdef5ab1fade3557377735a882b58.mp3#t=1838.24,1849.86)); current throughput is unestablished. The sample-holder correction anecdote (s00894–s00907, 2145.92–2176s) overlaps Latent Space s00232 and must not count as independent evidence. Commercial incentives are explicit in customer systems and retained-IP plans (s01206–s01223, [2848.96–2894.64s](https://api.substack.com/feed/podcast/215855083/2affdef5ab1fade3557377735a882b58.mp3#t=2848.96,2894.64)). Accept the distinct cost-scrutiny example, not repeated marketing, PhD-equivalence claims or established speedups; independent researchers and inspectable supporting results remain missing.",
      "piece_ids": ["02", "03"]
    }
  ]
}
